Manage Docker images — the blueprints used to create containers. Images are stored locally or in a 
registry


List all local Docker images
#docker images

List all images including intermediate layers
#docker images -a

Download an image from Docker Hub
#docker pull <image_name>

Pull a specific tag of an image
#docker pull <image_name>:<tag>

Push an image to a registry
#docker push <image_name>

Build an image from a Dockerfile in the current directory
#docker build -t <image_name>:<tag> .

Build an image using a specific Dockerfile
#docker build -f <Dockerfile_name> .

Build an image without using cache
#docker build --no-cache .

Pass build arguments to the Dockerfile
#docker build --build-arg <KEY>=<VALUE> .

Remove a Docker image
#docker rmi <image_name>

Forcefully remove a Docker image
#docker rmi -f <image_name>

Remove all dangling unused images
#docker image prune

Remove all unused Docker images
#docker image prune -a

Tag an image with a new name or tag
#docker tag <source_image>:<tag> <destination_image>:<tag>

Show layer history of an image
#docker history <image_name>

View detailed metadata of an image
#docker inspect <image_name>

Save a Docker image as a tar archive
#docker save -o <file_name>.tar <image_name>

Load a Docker image from a tar archive
#docker load -i <file_name>.tar

Import a filesystem tarball as a Docker image
#docker import <file_name>.tar <image_name>

Search Docker Hub for images
#docker search <search_term>

